{"archetypes":[{"name":"filesystem-toctou","focus_area":"security","weight":2,"rationale":"The new design_publish_ancestor_within_root helper is the primary security fix; its correctness determines whether the TOCTOU is actually closed, and edge cases in the cd-then-pwd-P pattern and case matching need deep analysis beyond generic security review.","prompt_body":"Focus on the new `design_publish_ancestor_within_root` helper in `scripts/design-log-publish.sh` and its three call sites (plan-review, render-cache, .completed staging loops). Verify that `cd \"$(dirname \"$_file\")\" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P` correctly fails closed when an ancestor directory is swapped for a symlink after the `find -type l` scan, and that the `case \"$_parent\" in \"$_root\"|\"$_root\"/*` pattern handles edge cases: dirname returning the root itself, trailing slashes on `_root`, and ancestor symlinks that point back inside the root (the plan says these should pass — confirm the case expression permits them). Identify any remaining race window between the ancestor check returning 0 and the subsequent `cp` inside `design_publish_stage_file`. Verify that the ancestor guard runs AFTER the per-file `[[ -L \"$f\" ]]` check and AFTER the plan-review allowlist check, per the plan's ordering note — a violation would mean the guard is unreachable for certain inputs. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"relay-completeness","focus_area":"security","weight":2,"rationale":"The audit in lib-quiet.md claims all high-risk relay paths are routed, but the static security reviewer will not enumerate every larch_err and larch_errf call site or verify the per-line sanitize_diagnostic_line wrap ordering is correct versus the redact-secrets.sh pass.","prompt_body":"Verify that the passthrough audit in `scripts/lib-quiet.md` accurately reflects the code changes in `scripts/ship-pr.sh`, `skills/review/scripts/collect-findings.sh`, `scripts/collect-agent-results.sh`, and `skills/review/scripts/review-core.sh`. For each `larch_err` and `larch_errf` call that relays external-sourced content (CI output, vendor stderr, subprocess log files), check that the wrap with `sanitize_diagnostic_line` is per-line (not applied to a multi-line string, which would strip embedded newlines) and that it runs AFTER any `redact-secrets.sh` pass, not before. Look specifically for `larch_errf` calls (not just `larch_err`) in these files that relay captured external content without sanitization, since the plan focuses on `larch_err "$line"` patterns. Check whether any of the ~40 static `print_usage` heredoc relays classified as LOW could in practice relay externally-influenced content at runtime. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"pre-rebase-commit-safety","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The new git add -u plus git-commit.sh block in run_rebase_rebump touches the commit graph in a safety-critical path; an accidentally staged file, an interaction with drop-bump-commit.sh Guards, or a pre-commit hook failure could corrupt the rebase sequence.","prompt_body":"Examine the new 'pre-rebase tracked-leftover fixup' block added to `scripts/ship-pr.sh` `run_rebase_rebump` (issue #3209). Verify that `git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no` scopes only to tracked modifications so `git add -u` cannot accidentally stage sensitive untracked artifacts from the session tmpdir or larch-logs. Check that the commit subject `'chore: pre-rebase working-tree fixup (#3209)'` does not match the bump-only or changelog-only patterns in `scripts/drop-bump-commit.sh` Guards 1–4, so this commit is never accidentally treated as a bump commit and dropped before the rebase. Verify that `failure_capture_path rebase` is called twice in the new block — check whether this generates a distinct path each invocation or overwrites the first failure log before `record_failure` reads it. Verify that when `git-commit.sh` fails (e.g., a pre-commit hook rejection leaves the index dirty), the subsequent fall-through to `drop-bump-commit.sh` Guard 1 produces a clean stall rather than a false-success path. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"mtime-behavioral-regression","focus_area":"edge-cases","weight":1,"rationale":"Switching /cleanup from depth-5 activity scan to top-level mtime alone is a silent behavioral regression that can delete active session directories written only deep inside; independent verification of blast radius and test coverage is needed.","prompt_body":"Focus on the cleanup behavioral change in `skills/cleanup/scripts/cleanup.sh`: the depth-5 `find -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 5` newest-descendant scan has been replaced with top-level mtime only. Verify that `skills/cleanup/scripts/test-cleanup.sh` actually tests the new semantics — specifically a session directory whose top-level mtime is older than the retention window but whose deep contents are recent (this was protected before and should now be deleted; the test must assert the new behavior, not the old). Check whether the `2>/dev/null` on `find` enumeration and `|| true` on the read loop silently suppress errors so a failed enumeration produces zero deletions that are indistinguishable from 'nothing to clean', and whether the operator has any visibility into this. Verify that the `/tmp` larch-pattern matching in the age pass still uses the same name-pattern scope as before the change and has not inadvertently broadened. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
